{"id":"work-order-paying-departments","title":"Departments whose internal charges grew faster than their budgets","question":"In which departments did what they are charged by other departments grow much faster than their own day-to-day spending between fiscal 2019 and 2026?","finding":"Of 30 departments charged $5 million or more by other departments in fiscal 2026, 12 saw those charges grow at least 25 points faster than their own day-to-day spending: Emergency Management (charges 155%, own spending 62%; largest rise in the line “GF-Social Services”, $14.3 million); Juvenile Probation (charges 129%, own spending 37%; largest rise in the line “GF-Children;Youth;& Family Svc”, $2.6 million); District Attorney (charges 94%, own spending 38%; largest rise in the line “Leases Paid To Real Estate”, $7.0 million); Fine Arts Museum (charges 63%, own spending 7.0%; largest rise in the line “GF-PUC-Light Heat & Power”, $1.3 million); Retirement System (charges 43%, own spending -0.8%; largest rise in the line “GF-City Attorney-Legal Service”, $1.3 million). 5 went the other way. The largest buyer is Public Health, charged $210.8 million in fiscal 2026 (5.9% of its day-to-day spending).","how":"For each department: spending lines of the kind “Services Of Other Depts” in fiscal 2019 and fiscal 2026, set against the department’s day-to-day spending (salaries, benefits, purchased services, supplies, grants, aid and the internal charges themselves) in the same two years. “Gap” is the growth of the charges less the growth of that spending. The line named is the sub-account, as the ledger names it, that added the most dollars. Departments charged under $5 million in fiscal 2026 are left out. Sorted by the gap.","limits":"A charge can jump because work moved, not because a price rose: a function transferred to another department, a new building whose rent is now billed centrally, a programme one department funds and another runs. The audit of April 2025 gives an example of the last kind that outlived its reason (a child-care subsidy paid by one department and handed out by another). A buyer has little say over centrally set charges such as power, workers’ compensation and technology. Two single years are compared, so a one-off in either distorts the picture. What would settle it: the agreement and bills behind the line that rose.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"department","t":"Department charged","type":"text"},{"k":"then","t":"Charged in fiscal 2019","type":"usd"},{"k":"now","t":"Charged in fiscal 2026","type":"usd"},{"k":"growth","t":"Growth of charges","type":"pct"},{"k":"ownGrowth","t":"Growth of its day-to-day spending","type":"pct"},{"k":"gap","t":"Gap","type":"pct"},{"k":"shareThen","t":"Share of its spending, 2019","type":"pct"},{"k":"shareNow","t":"Share, 2026","type":"pct"},{"k":"biggestRise","t":"Line that rose most, as the ledger names it","type":"text"},{"k":"biggestRiseDollars","t":"Dollars added on it","type":"usd"}],"rows":[{"department":"Emergency Management","then":9876432,"now":25206394,"growth":1.5522,"ownGrowth":0.6227,"gap":0.9295,"shareThen":0.1175,"shareNow":0.1849,"biggestRise":"GF-Social Services","biggestRiseDollars":14327467},{"department":"Juvenile Probation","then":3316499,"now":7588019,"growth":1.288,"ownGrowth":0.3736,"gap":0.9144,"shareThen":0.0939,"shareNow":0.1564,"biggestRise":"GF-Children;Youth;& Family Svc","biggestRiseDollars":2634984},{"department":"District Attorney","then":7434580,"now":14442886,"growth":0.9427,"ownGrowth":0.3812,"gap":0.5614,"shareThen":0.1084,"shareNow":0.1525,"biggestRise":"Leases Paid To Real Estate","biggestRiseDollars":6997543},{"department":"Fine Arts Museum","then":3426821,"now":5579007,"growth":0.628,"ownGrowth":0.0703,"gap":0.5577,"shareThen":0.1458,"shareNow":0.2218,"biggestRise":"GF-PUC-Light Heat & Power","biggestRiseDollars":1311116},{"department":"Retirement System","then":6539535,"now":9350996,"growth":0.4299,"ownGrowth":-0.0075,"gap":0.4374,"shareThen":0.1001,"sha